docs/backlog.md:103:Column 0 of the three tables takes `datatable.IndicatorColumn`, like Images (D101). Decisions: (1) *a remove, login or logout in flight is the table's `busy` marker*, reported as `Running` with its label; its failure stays a footer `Error` (D5). (2) *`refreshingGroups` is a row state*: a refreshing group is `Running` on its Registries row and the Members cell is static dim text; `WantsBlink()` is true only while a group refreshes or a row action runs, so the periodic refresh alone never blinks. (3) *the chain goes*: `advanceBusySpinners`, `busyTick`, `spinnerFrameIdx` and the `AdvanceSpinner` calls are deleted; the view's `spinner.Model` turns only while a tab loads (footer, Rule 128). `datatable.AdvanceSpinner` and `StatusColumn` stay in the package until A-16 (criterion 7). The OCI registry browser is still pending (A-09a2, DEVDESK-218). Details in `docs/architecture/network.md`.
docs/backlog.md:107:Column 0 takes `datatable.IndicatorColumn`; the row spinner, `busyTick` and the `AdvanceSpinner` exception are deleted. Decisions: (Q1-A) `docker.ParseStatus` reads the `Status` column of `ps` once ("Up 3 hours", "Exited (137) 5 minutes ago"): exact exit code, age as the lower bound of docker's rounded duration, restart count unavailable; rejected: `docker inspect` per refresh (one more subprocess every 2 s, a template to check under podman, a new failure path for the list). Text it does not recognise yields no code and no age, so an exit with an unknown code is `Failed` `⊗`, never `⊖` (a possible crash is not shown as clean). (Q2-A) a running container shows `theme.IconContainer` in the new `IconRoleContainer` role (theme key `icon_container`, default `ColorText`). (Q3-C) podman's `stopped` reads like `exited`; any other state outside the vocabulary shows `◌` and the footer names it as reported. (Q4-A) the selected row is red only for `Failed`. (Q5-B) **amends D10 of the activity-indicator stories** (criterion 8 of A-14): the legend entry reads "an action (stop, restart, pause, resume, delete) is running", since this view has no kill.
docs/backlog.md:151:Until now the router ran one spinner chain (`jobTickMsg`) that broadcast a full `ChangedMsg` (snapshot and frame) to every held view at 10 Hz. The story asks for one tick that animates every row, blinking at 1 Hz, without rebroadcasting the snapshot. Decisions: (1) *One chain, renamed "animation"* (D2; formerly D5's "spinner chain"): `animationTickMsg{seq, at}`, `ensureTick`, `handleAnimationTick`, `animationState`. Only the router builds a `jobs.FrameMsg`; a source test enforces it. (2) *Two cadences:* 100 ms while `Running() > 0` (footer and row spinners need the frame), 500 ms when the view on screen implements `BlinkingView` and `WantsBlink()` is true and motion is blink. Otherwise no chain. (3) *The phase is read from the tick's own time:* `blinkOn = (UnixMilli/500) % 2 == 0`, so every row reads the same phase from the same tick. (4) *`FrameMsg{Frame, RenderedFrame, Blink}`* goes to the view on screen only, on every tick and on entry (`switchView`). `Frame` and `RenderedFrame` leave `jobs.ChangedMsg`, which keeps the snapshot only and now goes to every held view on a registry change. This reverses the earlier "one message and not two" comment, on purpose: the compiler then lists every reader of the frame, which protects against a view whose spinner freezes. (5) *Level signal:* `App.Update` is now a wrapper (`a.update` is today's switch, unchanged, then `ensureTick`). `ensureTick` runs after every message: it starts a chain when one is needed, replaces a slower chain by bumping `seq` (never two chains), and lets a live chain die at its next tick when nothing needs it. (6) *`tea.Every`, not `tea.Tick`:* `tea.Every` fires on the clock's boundaries, so `(UnixMilli/500) % 2` alternates strictly. With `tea.Tick` the drift accumulates and one blink is skipped (a steady 1 s `●`). (7) *Only the view on screen is asked `WantsBlink()`*, not every held view as the story's R3 said. Reason: the frame goes to the view on screen only, so a chain kept alive for an off-screen view would animate nothing. Stricter than the story; reversible in one line. (8) *`motionStatic` is a router field until A-02:* `setMotionStatic(bool)`, zero value = blink. A-02 wires it from `app.motion`; nothing is read from YAML here. `app.motion` does not exist yet (no `Motion` in `internal/config`). (9) *`datatable.Animating()` is not added here:* it has no caller before the first `BlinkingView` (A-09b/A-10a/A-13/A-14), and it goes with it. (10) *Four row-animation chains stay outside the router, declared as exceptions until A-16:* `oci_resources/busy.go` (`AdvanceSpinner`, A-09b), `containers/update.go` (`AdvanceSpinner`, A-14), `netdiag/ports_model.go` (`portsSpinnerCmd`, A-12), the OCI registry browser's `tagScanSpinnerIdx` (A-09a). A test lists them as declared exceptions: a new site fails it, and a stale exception fails it too. Criterion 4 of the story is therefore met on the router side here, and application-wide in A-16 (Q2, option A). (11) *Redraw-cost measurement is an in-process test, not a terminal capture* (Q1, option A): `TestMeasureRedrawCost` in `internal/app/redraw_measure_test.go`, skipped unless `DEVDESK_MEASURE_REDRAW=1`, runs a real `tea.Program` on the real router with `WithOutput(countingWriter)`, 120×40, for 10 s, and logs bytes per second for N = 0, 1, 20 blinking rows. The story named `TEA_TRACE`, which does not exist in Bubble Tea v1.3.10 (checked in the module source: no `TEA_TRACE`, no `os.Getenv`); the research document cited it from a page about another version. This measures what the renderer writes, not what a terminal shows over SSH; the real-SSH check stays with the A-06 checkpoint, where H2 is consigned. No view blinks before A-06, so the PR cannot show "20 blinking rows" in the real application. (12) *No ADR:* a lasting decision recorded here, per the project's convention. Hand-off: A-02 calls `setMotionStatic`; A-06 adds `SetBlinkPhase(msg.Blink)` to `:jobs`; the first `BlinkingView` adds `datatable.Animating()`; A-16 absorbs the declared exceptions.
